const hobbesArticle: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'orientation', title: 'A philosophy of bodies, words, and public authority', paragraphs: [
    'Thomas Hobbes is often introduced through one frightening sentence about life without government. That shortcut misses the architecture of his philosophy. He sought explanations of sensation, imagination, language, reasoning, motion, law, religious conflict, and political authority within a broadly materialist account of nature. Leviathan begins with sense and speech before reaching covenant and sovereignty because a commonwealth is made from human agents whose desires, fears, judgments, and words must be coordinated. Political order is artificial, but it is constructed from natural powers and vulnerabilities rather than floating above them.',
    'Hobbes’s governing problem is not that every person is constantly murderous. It is that agents with roughly comparable vulnerability, uncertain intentions, competing judgments, and no acknowledged public arbiter can fall into defensive conflict. The remedy is not mere terror imposed from outside. People authorize a representative whose public decisions count as theirs, creating a common power able to define law and secure performance. This solution is severe and contested. It also raises the enduring questions of whether authorization can justify concentrated power, what subjects still retain, and when protection and obligation come apart.',
    'Reading this architecture as a whole also prevents two opposite distortions. Hobbes is neither a theorist who thinks force by itself creates a legitimate state nor a familiar liberal who treats political power as a revocable service contract. Fear can make submission reasonable, but authorization explains whose act the sovereign performs; natural laws direct people toward peace, but enforcement makes mutual restraint dependable; protection grounds obedience, but subjects cannot simply dissolve public judgment whenever they privately expect a better outcome. The conceptual pieces support one another while leaving a real danger: the institution designed to overcome insecure private power concentrates coercive and interpretive authority in a representative whom subjects normally cannot call to account. That unresolved combination—constructed legitimacy, overwhelming authority, and retained bodily liberty—is why Hobbes remains useful to theorists who reject his political conclusions. It also makes representation, not a caricature of human wickedness, the best entry point into his politics.'
  ]},
  {id: 'life-context', title: 'Civil war, exile, controversy, and a long intellectual life', paragraphs: [
    'Born in 1588, Hobbes was educated at Oxford and spent much of his career connected to the Cavendish family. Travel and intellectual networks brought him into contact with continental mathematics, optics, and natural philosophy. He translated Thucydides, debated free will with Bishop Bramhall, disputed mathematics with John Wallis, and wrote on history as well as philosophy. His political works developed through The Elements of Law, De Cive, the English Leviathan of 1651, and later Latin revisions. This was an evolving program, not one timeless argument extracted from a single chapter.',
    'The wars of the three kingdoms, parliamentary conflict, exile in Paris, censorship, and disputes over church authority are indispensable context. Yet it is too simple to say that fear of civil war mechanically produced absolutism. Hobbes was already developing a causal and demonstrative philosophy, and he connected political instability to disagreement over names, doctrines, judgment, and rival claims to represent the people or speak for God. His own religious commitments remain contested. What is secure is that he treated religious belief and ecclesiastical jurisdiction as politically powerful, not as decorative material appended to a secular theory.'
  ]},
  {id: 'materialism-mind', title: 'Materialism without a crude machine metaphor', paragraphs: [
    'Hobbes denies that an immaterial substance is needed to explain sensation or thought. External bodies produce motions in sense organs; those motions continue inward, and appearances arise in the sentient body. Imagination is decaying sense, while trains of thought follow patterns of association shaped by desire and prior experience. This is materialism in the sense that whatever exists is body and that mental activity belongs to bodily nature. The details are not modern neuroscience, and Hobbes does not possess an experimental account of neural representation. His explanatory wager is historical and philosophical: motion and organization can do work previously assigned to incorporeal forms.',
    'This materialism does not reduce human life to a single appetite for survival. Hobbes analyzes desire and aversion, pleasure and pain, hope, fear, glory, curiosity, and the restless pursuit of power as diverse motions and orientations. Deliberation is the succession of competing appetites and aversions ending in will. Freedom, in his compatibility framework, concerns the absence of external impediments rather than an uncaused power to choose. Critics ask whether this leaves enough room for responsibility; defenders emphasize that actions still issue from the agent’s own deliberation. Neither side should replace Hobbes’s account with the slogan that people are naturally selfish robots.'
  ]},
  {id: 'language-science', title: 'Names, reckoning, and the ambition of science', paragraphs: [
    'Speech allows people to register thoughts, communicate, make covenants, and reason by adding and subtracting consequences attached to names. It also creates new dangers: meaningless combinations, equivocation, metaphor treated as proof, and contested definitions can produce apparent knowledge or political disorder. Hobbes is often called a nominalist because universality belongs to names rather than to universal things existing outside minds. But naming is not arbitrary private labeling. Stable definitions and public use make reasoning possible, while badly governed words permit authorities and factions to manipulate judgment.',
    'Hobbes admired geometry because definitions and constructions let reasoners demonstrate consequences from causes they understand. Civil philosophy can achieve a comparable certainty because humans make commonwealths and therefore can know their generative principles. Natural philosophy is harder: the causes of natural effects are not made by us and may be inferred only hypothetically from appearances. Scholars dispute how unified Hobbes’s method is across geometry, physics, psychology, and politics. It is safer to describe a family resemblance—causal explanation, definition, and ordered consequence—than to claim that Leviathan simply deduces politics from mechanics without historical observation or rhetoric.'
  ]},
  {id: 'state-nature', title: 'The state of nature as analytical condition, partial history, and recurring danger', paragraphs: [
    'The state of nature is a condition without a common power recognized as able to settle disputes. Equality here means enough equality of vulnerability and hope that even the stronger cannot securely dominate everyone. Scarcity, uncertainty, competition, diffidence, and concern for reputation make anticipatory violence rational under some conditions. A state of war need not contain uninterrupted fighting; it exists where there is a known disposition to fight and no dependable assurance of peace. Industry, navigation, secure property, and long-term cooperation become precarious because promises lack an enforcing public framework.',
    'Hobbes does not straightforwardly report that all humans once lived globally in isolated savagery. He offers examples involving civil war, relations among sovereigns, and peoples described through colonial reports, and those passages carry the ethnocentric assumptions of his setting. The argument also works hypothetically: remove effective public authority and examine what follows from private judgment under insecurity. Families, small groups, and unequal relations may exist before a commonwealth, so “state of nature” is not identical with solitary individuals. Its force lies in modeling a structural problem and a possibility that can recur when political authority collapses.'
  ]},
  {id: 'law-covenant', title: 'Fear, natural right, law of nature, and covenant', paragraphs: [
    'In the natural condition each person has a right, understood as liberty, to use available means for self-preservation. Reason nevertheless identifies laws of nature: seek peace when it can be had, lay down mutually dangerous liberties when others do likewise, and perform valid covenants. These precepts are not automatically effective civil laws. Without assurance and enforcement, a person cannot safely be expected to disarm while others retain the power to attack. Fear is therefore not merely a base passion that invalidates political agreement. Fear of violent death supplies a powerful reason to pursue peace and can coexist with rational consent.',
    'Interpreters disagree over whether Hobbesian obligation is fundamentally prudential, moral, divine, or some combination. Hobbes calls the laws of nature rational precepts and also presents them as divine laws when considered as God’s commands. Covenant changes normative relations, but covenant alone cannot stabilize itself in the absence of a coercive power. The famous social contract is thus not a contract between sovereign and people promising reciprocal performance. It is a covenant among prospective subjects to authorize one person or assembly, conditional upon sufficient others doing likewise, so that a public agent can act and judge in their name.'
  ]},
  {id: 'authorization-sovereignty', title: 'Authorization, representation, and undivided sovereignty', paragraphs: [
    'Leviathan’s account of persons distinguishes a natural person acting in their own name from an artificial person representing another. A multitude becomes one political person through authorization: subjects own the representative’s authorized public acts, even though they do not physically merge or share every private judgment. Institution creates sovereignty when people covenant to authorize a ruler or assembly; acquisition creates it under coercive conditions, which Hobbes argues can generate comparable obligations when submission secures life. This analysis makes representation constitutive of the state, not merely a later electoral technique.',
    'The sovereign must possess final authority over law, war, peace, public doctrine, judicature, offices, and the conditions of property. Dividing final judgment among independent powers recreates the disputes the commonwealth was designed to end. Hobbes therefore rejects mixed sovereignty at the level of ultimate authority, though governments can use assemblies, ministers, courts, and local administration. His position is commonly called absolutist, but absolute does not mean omnipotent or morally infallible. A sovereign can govern badly, misunderstand public good, and lack practical capacity. The harder claim is that subjects cannot reserve a general public right to judge and punish the sovereign without dissolving authorization.'
  ]},
  {id: 'obligation-limits', title: 'What subjects owe—and what they never gave away', paragraphs: [
    'Political obligation is extensive because peace requires a settled public judgment. Yet subjects do not covenant away the inescapable liberty to resist direct threats to life, bodily integrity, or necessary means of survival. A person ordered to kill or wound themself, refrain from food or medicine, or confess a crime without assurance of pardon may refuse. Soldiers can sometimes flee from fear even when the sovereign may punish them. These liberties are not a liberal bill of rights against government; they arise because no covenant can obligate a person not to preserve themself when the decisive moment comes.',
    'Obligation also depends on protection. Hobbes states that the end of obedience is protection and that obligation lasts while sovereign power can protect. What counts as failure, and whether subjects may collectively judge it, remain difficult. He does not license rebellion whenever a law seems unjust or policy imprudent. Nor does his theory erase every moral assessment of rulers. The result is a tension: authority must be sufficiently decisive to solve coordination and insecurity, but the grounds for authorizing it—preservation and peace—also mark limits that no verbal transfer can remove. Later contract theories repeatedly reopen that tension.'
  ]},
  {id: 'religion', title: 'Scripture, prophecy, church power, and contested sincerity', paragraphs: [
    'Nearly half of Leviathan addresses Christian commonwealth and the “kingdom of darkness.” Hobbes analyzes prophecy, miracles, angels, spirits, scriptural language, ecclesiastical office, and the history of church power. He denies that private revelation can publicly authenticate commands against the sovereign and subjects doctrinal controversies to civil judgment. This is not a small secularizing footnote. Competing authorities who claim divine warrant can reactivate the state-of-nature problem inside a commonwealth, because subjects no longer know whose command is publicly binding. Control of public teaching is therefore part of sovereignty.',
    'Readers have interpreted Hobbes as an orthodox if unconventional Christian, a heterodox materialist revising Christianity, a skeptical critic writing under constraint, and several positions between. The texts do not settle the sincerity question by a single confession. His materialist treatment of spirit, restrictive account of supernatural knowledge, and political subordination of churches were scandalous, but he also devotes sustained argument to scripture and Christian obligation. A careful article records the interpretive dispute and distinguishes it from the secure institutional point: independent clerical jurisdiction and unauthorized prophetic claims threaten the unity of public authority in Hobbes’s analysis.'
  ]},
  {id: 'reception-reading', title: 'What Hobbes changes, and how to read him without slogans', paragraphs: [
    'Hobbes transformed social-contract argument, theories of representation, accounts of sovereignty, legal positivist debates, materialist psychology, and modern discussions of security. Locke and Rousseau defined parts of their political projects against him; later theorists have used his state of nature in game theory, international relations, and theories of public goods. Those receptions often isolate one mechanism and discard his theology, language theory, or science. His influence is strongest when the whole problem remains visible: vulnerable interpreters need common rules, yet the institution empowered to settle conflict can itself become dangerous.',
    'Begin with Leviathan chapters 1–6 on sense, imagination, speech, reason, and passions; then read chapters 13–21 as one argument from insecurity through authorization to subjects’ liberty. Continue with chapters 26, 29–31, 33, 36–38, and 42 to see civil law and religion rather than ending after the social contract. Use the general, science, and moral-political SEP entries to separate disputed interpretations, then consult the Cambridge companions for deeper disagreements. The best beginner question is not whether Hobbes “liked dictatorship,” but what kind of public authority can turn fearful coexistence into dependable cooperation without claiming more than authorization can give.'
  ]},
];

