const berkeleyArticle: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'orientation', title: 'Immaterialism as a defense of the ordinary world', paragraphs: [
    'George Berkeley argues that sensible objects are not material substances existing unperceived behind experience. They are collections of ideas perceived by spirits and ordered with a stability that does not depend on any one finite observer. This view is commonly called immaterialism or idealism. It can sound like the claim that nothing is real, but Berkeley presents it as an attack on skepticism: the table is the color, shape, resistance, and other sensible content encountered according to regular conditions, not an unknowable material support hidden behind all possible experience. He wants ordinary objects restored, not dissolved.',
    'The cost of that restoration is a radical ontology. Ideas are passive objects of perception; spirits are active perceivers and agents; God explains the involuntary, coherent order of sensory ideas. Berkeley rejects matter rather than reducing everything to a private stream of consciousness. Whether this really preserves common sense is disputed. Critics ask how objects persist, how other minds are known, whether spirit resembles the obscure substance he rejects, and whether divine causation explains too much. His philosophy matters because it makes those familiar assumptions visible rather than because one famous Latin formula ends every objection.',
    'The familiar formula “to be is to be perceived” therefore needs two qualifications. It concerns sensible things and ideas, not spirits, which exist as perceivers and agents rather than as perceived bundles. It also does not mean that an object vanishes whenever one human looks away: the ordered sensory world is not dependent on a particular finite mind, and Berkeley appeals to divine perception and causation to explain its continuity and lawlike presentation. Those commitments create further problems rather than a cheap escape. They make theology part of the ontology, require an account of how finite spirits are known, and shift scientific explanation from hidden material powers toward stable relations among the ideas nature presents. In that framework, laws of nature remain indispensable for prediction and practical knowledge even though they describe reliable signs rather than efficient powers residing in mind-independent matter. The result is a philosophy of science as well as a provocation about perception, with genuine explanatory ambitions and genuine theological liabilities that remain live today.'
  ]},
  {id: 'life-works', title: 'Ireland, Trinity College, the church, and an unusually wide corpus', paragraphs: [
    'Berkeley was born near Kilkenny in 1685, studied and taught at Trinity College Dublin, entered Anglican orders, traveled in Europe, spent several years in Rhode Island while pursuing a proposed colonial college, and later became Bishop of Cloyne. His early notebooks record engagement with Locke, Descartes, Malebranche, Newton, Hobbes, and contemporary mathematics. An Essay towards a New Theory of Vision appeared in 1709, the Principles in 1710, and Three Dialogues in 1713. Later works address motion, free-thinking, mathematics, economics, medicine, and the visual language of God.',
    'The biography complicates the canonical picture of three “British empiricists” in a straight line. Berkeley was an Irish-born philosopher and Church of Ireland cleric working inside Anglo-Irish institutions and British imperial networks. His religious aims were central: immaterialism was meant to oppose skepticism and atheism, while perception and nature displayed providential order. Yet religion did not keep him from technical inquiry. His theory of vision, De Motu, critique of infinitesimals in The Analyst, economic questions in The Querist, and speculative chain of reflections in Siris show a changing program that cannot be reduced to Principles section 3.'
  ]},
  {id: 'abstraction', title: 'The attack on abstract ideas and misleading words', paragraphs: [
    'The Introduction to the Principles attacks a theory of abstraction according to which the mind forms an idea of, for example, extension with no particular shape or size, or a triangle that is neither equilateral, isosceles, nor scalene. Berkeley grants that we can attend selectively, use one particular idea to represent many, and reason generally. What he denies is the need for an intrinsically abstract image containing incompatible or wholly indeterminate features. Generality belongs to the use of signs, not to a ghostly mental picture stripped of every particular condition.',
    'This argument has several roles. It challenges theories of material substance that ask us to abstract extension, motion, and other qualities from every perceivable particular. It also supports Berkeley’s account of mathematics and warns that words can appear meaningful after the ideas supposedly attached to them disappear. Scholars disagree about the exact target and whether anti-abstractionism independently establishes immaterialism or only removes a defense of matter. It should not be summarized as hostility to general thought. Berkeley needs general reasoning; he relocates it in sign-use, selective attention, and the representative function of particulars.'
  ]},
  {id: 'ideas-objects', title: 'Ideas, ordinary objects, and what “to be is to be perceived” means', paragraphs: [
    'Sensible qualities such as colors, sounds, tastes, shapes, and resistance are ideas: they exist as perceived. Berkeley argues that primary qualities cannot be placed in a mind-independent material bearer while secondary qualities remain in the mind. Shape and motion vary with perspective and scale; an idea can resemble only another idea; and a supposed material substratum is not itself sensibly characterized. Ordinary objects are organized collections of sensible ideas. An apple is not one private image but a lawful cluster of visual, tactile, gustatory, and other ideas available under appropriate conditions.',
    'The formula esse est percipi applies directly to ideas, not to spirits, which exist as perceivers and agents. Nor does it say that an object exists only when one particular human looks at it. Berkeley can appeal to other finite perceivers, counterfactual availability, and ultimately God’s continuous ordering of nature. His accounts shift between the Principles and Dialogues, producing debate about unperceived objects. The philosophical pressure remains: if every feature used to identify a sensible object belongs to possible experience, what explanatory work is performed by an additional wholly unperceived material support?'
  ]},
  {id: 'spirits-god', title: 'Active spirits, notions, and divine causation', paragraphs: [
    'Ideas are passive: a color or pain does not cause another idea simply by being perceived. Spirits, by contrast, understand and will. Berkeley says we have no idea of spirit because an active subject cannot resemble a passive perceptual item. Later editions use “notion” to mark a non-imagistic grasp of ourselves, relations, and God. This creates a demanding asymmetry. Matter is rejected partly because it cannot be represented by an idea, yet spirit is retained without an idea. Berkeley answers that agency and self-awareness give positive grounds for spirit that no inert material cause possesses. Critics dispute whether the distinction succeeds.',
    'Because sensory ideas arrive with a vividness, regularity, and independence from finite will unlike imagination, Berkeley infers an ordering spirit. God causes the natural sequence of ideas according to general rules. Finite spirits cause volitions and bodily actions in a derivative sense, though explaining the relation between will and sensory result is difficult. God does not merely watch every tree so it stays existent; divine activity grounds the intelligible system in which experiences are coordinated. The theological argument is meant to secure realism and providence, but it raises familiar problems about evil, irregularity, other minds, and the explanatory distance between a regular sequence and an infinite cause.'
  ]},
  {id: 'vision-signs', title: 'Learning to see distance and reading nature as language', paragraphs: [
    'In the New Theory of Vision Berkeley argues that distance, magnitude, and spatial relations are not immediately given by sight in the same way as colors and light. Visual cues acquire meaning through experience of correlations with touch, movement, and bodily consequences. We learn that certain visual patterns signify how far away something is or what tactile encounter to expect. The theory is historically situated and not identical with contemporary vision science, but its central insight is developmental: perception that feels immediate can depend on learned associations among sensory modalities.',
    'Berkeley later develops this into a doctrine of visual language. Visual ideas signify tactile ideas without resembling them, much as words signify meanings by established use. Nature is therefore a system of signs through which God instructs finite agents about what they may encounter and how to act. This model also clarifies his broader philosophy of language: representation need not depend on pictorial likeness. It does not make natural regularities arbitrary moment-to-moment messages. A language works through dependable patterns, and science expands our grasp of those patterns even if it does not discover inert efficient causes behind them.'
  ]},
  {id: 'science', title: 'Science after matter: laws, mechanisms, and useful prediction', paragraphs: [
    'Berkeley does not think immaterialism abolishes natural science. Scientists identify regularities, analogies, structures, and rules among phenomena. A mechanical explanation can connect a complex event to general laws and hidden microstructures understood as possible sensible configurations, even though matter is not an efficient cause. The true efficient cause of sensory ideas is spirit; natural laws describe the stable grammar of divine production. This distinguishes metaphysical causation from the explanatory practices of physics. The result is sometimes called instrumentalism, though that label can understate Berkeley’s realism about the experienced natural order.',
    'De Motu treats force, attraction, and related terms as mathematically useful hypotheses rather than names for occult qualities literally residing in bodies. Berkeley accepts the predictive and organizing achievements of Newtonian mechanics while resisting metaphysical reification. In The Analyst he attacks the logical foundations used in calculus, especially reasoning with fluxions and vanishing quantities, partly to challenge free-thinkers who mocked religious mysteries. Later mathematics answered those worries in new ways. His critique was not a refusal of mathematics; it was a demand that its signs, operations, and inferential licenses be explained with the clarity philosophers demanded elsewhere.'
  ]},
  {id: 'common-sense', title: 'Idealism, realism, and the common-sense dispute', paragraphs: [
    'Berkeley’s Philonous repeatedly claims to defend common sense against the philosopher’s matter. We directly perceive houses, trees, stones, and bodies rather than internal copies from which an external cause must be inferred. Real things differ from dreams and fantasies through their coherence, vividness, involuntariness, and place in a public order. Error remains possible because ordinary claims involve expectations: saying that a cherry is red normally predicts how it will appear under standard light and to human observers. A strange appearance need not change every correct use of the object word.',
    'Whether this is realism depends on what one requires. Berkeley preserves mind-independent-of-me order and ordinary objects, but denies mind-independent sensible existence in an absolute sense. Calling him a subjective idealist can suggest that each subject manufactures a private world, which he rejects. Calling him simply a common-sense realist can hide the theological dependence of nature and his denial of material substance. The tension is productive: he exposes that “independent” can mean independent of my current perception, of all finite minds, of every mind whatsoever, or of conceptual and perceptual conditions. His view treats those meanings differently.'
  ]},
  {id: 'colonial-context', title: 'The Bermuda project, slaveholding, and institutional context', paragraphs: [
    'Berkeley’s American project cannot be confined to a charming failed college plan. He sought government funding for a college in Bermuda intended to educate clergy and advance missionary conversion in Britain’s colonies. He lived in Rhode Island from 1729 to 1731 while awaiting funds that did not arrive, purchased a farm, and enslaved several people in his household. Documentary evidence identifies purchases and a baptismal record while leaving parts of those individuals’ lives unrecoverable. His writings also defended forms of Christian instruction for enslaved people without challenging slavery as an institution.',
    'This context is materially relevant to Berkeley’s institutional and religious thought. The project joined education, empire, evangelization, property, and racial slavery; it was not entailed by immaterialism, yet it belonged to the same historical agent’s program. Later gifts and intellectual relationships connected his name to American colleges, and institutions have reexamined that commemoration. The evidence should neither be hidden behind abstract metaphysics nor used to pretend that one colonial episode deductively refutes his theory of perception. It changes the ethical and historical understanding of what his educational and providential ambitions meant in practice.'
  ]},
  {id: 'reception-reading', title: 'A disciplined route through Berkeley’s arguments and afterlives', paragraphs: [
    'Berkeley shaped Hume’s discussions of abstraction and perception, became a target for Reid’s common-sense philosophy and Kant’s distinction from “dogmatic idealism,” and remains central to debates about perception, realism, signs, causation, and scientific explanation. Later caricatures ask whether he kicked stones out of existence or anticipated quantum mechanics. Neither helps. Physical impact is itself a forceful tactile idea within his view, and quantum “observation” is not Berkeleyan perception by a spirit. The continuing value lies in his challenge to specify what matter explains and how ordinary-object talk relates to experience.',
    'Read the Principles Introduction and sections 1–33 first, then sections 34–84 on objections and 85–156 on knowledge, science, and God. Use the Three Dialogues to see how Berkeley reformulates the case around common sense. Add the New Theory of Vision and De Motu before judging his science. The SEP entry and Cambridge Companion map disputes over abstraction, ordinary objects, spirits, and agency; Winkler offers a sustained interpretation. Finally, read the Trinity and Yale historical reviews alongside Berkeley’s colonial proposal. A complete encounter holds metaphysical ingenuity, religious purpose, and colonial responsibility in the same frame without collapsing their differences.'
  ]},
];

