const conwayArticle: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'orientation', title: 'A living creation against inert matter and mind–body division', paragraphs: [
    'Anne Conway builds a metaphysics in which created reality is alive, perceptive, mobile, and capable of moral and bodily transformation. She rejects the Cartesian division between immaterial thinking substance and inert extended matter because radically different substances cannot explain their intimate interaction. She also rejects Hobbesian materialism and what she takes to be Spinoza’s confusion of God with creatures. Her alternative is not the claim that everything is one undifferentiated mind. It is a graded, relational world of spiritual substance whose embodied forms can become subtler or grosser while remaining within created nature.',
    'The system answers several problems at once. A perfectly good God should not create dead substance incapable of receiving life; individual creatures need enough continuity to change and be responsible; suffering and evil require an account compatible with divine justice; and mind–body interaction should not depend on contact between wholly alien kinds. Conway’s solution joins metaphysics, theology, ethics, and natural philosophy. Its unfamiliar terms—three species, middle nature, spirit, body, and perfectibility—must be learned on their own terms before they are translated into Leibnizian monads, modern panpsychism, or generic vitalism.',
    'That caution matters because several modern labels illuminate only part of the design. “Monism” can name the shared kind of created substance, yet it can obscure Conway’s immutable God, mediating Christ, and plurality of creatures. “Vitalism” captures the rejection of dead matter, yet it can conceal the moral and theological direction of transformation. “Panpsychism” notices perception throughout nature, yet it can flatten her hierarchy of organized living centers into the claim that every smallest item has the same mentality. Her project is strongest when its connections stay visible: ontology makes interaction possible, embodiment records mutable life, ethics gives change a direction, and divine justice makes restoration rather than endless punishment the governing horizon. The same discipline applies to historical comparisons. Her affinities with Leibniz are genuine and connected by books and networks, but they do not turn the Principles into a preliminary Monadology. Her opposition to Descartes, Hobbes, and Spinoza is philosophically productive even where her descriptions of their views are contestable. Conway should be read as an author constructing a rival system, not merely as a missing link between more familiar men. Her posthumous transmission complicates exact wording without diminishing that authorship.'
  ]},
  {id: 'life-network', title: 'Philosophy through correspondence, household, illness, and friendship', paragraphs: [
    'Born Anne Finch in 1631, Conway gained an unusual philosophical education through her half-brother John Finch and correspondence with Henry More, who became her tutor and intellectual interlocutor. She married Edward Conway and lived much of her adult life at Ragley Hall. Chronic, severe headaches led to consultations with physicians and healers and connected questions of body, spirit, medicine, and suffering to daily experience. Her household became a node linking Cambridge Platonists, experimental medicine, religious dissent, and European intellectual exchange rather than a private retreat outside seventeenth-century philosophy.',
    'Francis Mercury van Helmont became especially important in her later years, introducing Kabbalistic sources in a Christianized form and participating in medical and religious networks. Conway also encountered Quakers and converted near the end of her life. Neither fact supplies a simple key that explains every doctrine. Her philosophy emerged through sustained engagement with More, Descartes, Hobbes, Spinoza, Origenist themes, Platonism, Kabbalah, medicine, and lived religious practice. Treating her only as More’s gifted pupil erases her criticism of his dualism; treating her as an isolated genius erases the correspondence and collaborative setting that made her work possible.'
  ]},
  {id: 'text-mediation', title: 'One posthumous treatise and a difficult chain of transmission', paragraphs: [
    'Conway’s only surviving philosophical treatise was probably composed in the late 1670s and not published during her lifetime. Her English manuscript was transcribed from difficult handwriting, translated into Latin, and anonymously printed in Amsterdam in 1690 as Principia philosophiae antiquissimae et recentissimae. An English translation from that Latin appeared in 1692. The original English manuscript is lost. Modern editions therefore do not present untouched authorial copy: they reconstruct Conway through a posthumous transcription, translation, editorial division, and retranslation that can affect terminology and syntax.',
    'This mediation matters especially when the text sounds systematic. Chapter and section numbers are indispensable locators, but they should not imply that Conway authorized every printed choice or preface. The published preface praises an unnamed learned countess and frames the work for readers after her death. Van Helmont and others facilitated transmission; their roles do not make them coauthors of her philosophical positions. A careful reading distinguishes Conway’s arguments in the treatise from editorial testimony about its origin, from her letters, and from later comparisons. Attribution is strongest at the level of the treatise’s connected doctrines, more cautious at the level of exact lost English wording.'
  ]},
  {id: 'three-species', title: 'God, Christ or middle nature, and creatures', paragraphs: [
    'Conway distinguishes three species of being by their capacity for change. God is wholly immutable, infinitely good, wise, just, and the source of being. Christ, or middle nature, mediates between God and creatures and changes only toward greater good, never toward evil. Creatures can change toward better or worse. These boundaries cannot be crossed: a creature does not become God or Christ through improvement. The hierarchy protects divine transcendence while allowing creation to receive and communicate life through mediation. It is theological architecture, not a secular scale of complexity with religious labels attached.',
    'Whether this makes Conway a monist, dualist, or “trialist” is disputed. All created beings are of one living spiritual kind, which supports type-monist readings of creation. Yet God, Christ, and creatures are three essentially distinct species, and individual creatures retain plurality, which resists existence monism like the view she attributes to Spinoza. Jessica Gordon-Roth maps these options and argues that labels depend on what the oneness ranges over. The safest account is explicit: Conway unifies created substance while maintaining a three-species hierarchy and real creaturely plurality. No single modern category should silently replace those commitments.'
  ]},
  {id: 'created-substance', title: 'Spiritual substance, individuality, and unity in multiplicity', paragraphs: [
    'Created substance is living and capable of perception and motion. Conway sometimes describes one created substance comprising innumerable spirits or “species,” language that has fueled disputes over whether there is one created world-substance or many individual substances within an ordered unity. Her examples emphasize communication and composition: creatures are internally multiple, organized, and related rather than simple points sealed off from one another. Individuality remains ethically important because creatures improve or deteriorate and bear consequences, yet their identity belongs within larger living unities.',
    'This ontology rejects both dead extension and an absolute gulf between minds. If every creature is spiritual in kind, interaction no longer requires an immaterial mind to push an alien material body. Finer and grosser modes can affect one another because they belong to a continuum of created life. But “spirit” should not be mistaken for a disembodied ghost. Conway’s spirits have extension, motion, perception, and bodily expression. Her system is better approached as a metaphysics of living, organized powers than as a decision to call conventional matter by a religious word.'
  ]},
  {id: 'body-change', title: 'Body as condensed spirit and transformation as real process', paragraphs: [
    'Body is not a second inert substance. It is spirit made darker, grosser, or more resistant through creaturely change; conversely, embodied creatures can become subtler and more spiritual. This allows genuine transformation without generation from nothing or annihilation into nothing. The same creature can undergo profound bodily and moral alteration because its underlying kind is living and changeable. Conway therefore rejects atomism understood as permanently hard, indivisible, dead particles. A living body is divisible into living centers and belongs to nested organizations rather than being assembled from absolutely lifeless units.',
    'The language of condensation and rarefaction is analogical and metaphysical, not a modern physical measurement. Emily Thomas emphasizes the importance of process, time, and spatiality in understanding Conway’s world. Change is not an illusion placed over static substance; it is constitutive of created being. Nor is embodiment simply punishment to be discarded. Grosser embodiment can express moral decline and suffering, but bodily organization remains a mode of life through which creatures act, encounter consequences, and recover. Conway’s rejection of inert matter therefore deepens embodiment instead of replacing the natural world with private ideas.'
  ]},
  {id: 'evil-suffering', title: 'Evil, pain, justice, and the possibility of restoration', paragraphs: [
    'Because God is perfectly good and just, evil cannot be an independently created substance. Creatures were created good but can turn away from perfection through their own mutable activity. Moral decline corresponds to a grosser mode of life, while improvement brings greater harmony and subtlety. Pain functions as consequence and possible remedy: it can expose disorder and contribute to restoration rather than expressing divine cruelty. Conway rejects eternal punishment for finite wrongdoing as inconsistent with divine justice. Her theodicy is thus developmental and purgative, though the moral interpretation of suffering is troubling when applied without qualification to actual illness and oppression.',
    'Her own severe pain makes it tempting to read the entire system as autobiography, but the treatise offers arguments grounded in divine attributes and creaturely change, not a memoir explaining one patient’s symptoms. Nor should restoration be confused with guaranteed immediate comfort. Creatures can move repeatedly toward better or worse through long transformations. Conway wants both moral responsibility and hope: difference between good and evil is real, consequences are real, and no finite lapse justifies endless, purposeless torment. The unresolved question is whether a purgative account can explain suffering proportionately without making every injury a deserved instrument of improvement.'
  ]},
  {id: 'religious-sources', title: 'Platonism, Origen, Kabbalah, Quakerism, and disciplined influence claims', paragraphs: [
    'Conway’s system draws on a religiously plural intellectual environment. Henry More transmitted Cambridge Platonist and Origenist concerns; Van Helmont brought a Christianized Kabbalah; Quaker practices and theology mattered late in her life; and the treatise uses Christian claims about God, Christ, creation, and redemption. Similarity is not proof of a single source. Kabbalistic themes of living emanation and restoration are important, but the materials reached Conway through mediation. Origenist perfectibility and rejection of eternal punishment offer another strand. The resulting philosophy is an original synthesis and critique rather than a faithful restatement of one tradition.',
    'Her conversion to Quakerism should likewise not be projected backward as the cause of every earlier idea. Quaker emphasis on inward divine life and spiritual equality resonated with her later commitments, while her social position and metaphysical vocabulary remained historically specific. The best evidence distinguishes chronology: correspondence and Hutton’s biography establish changing relationships; the Principles establishes the final connected system; source comparisons show possibilities and affinities. This avoids two symmetrical errors—secularizing her until theology becomes metaphor, or treating religious affiliation as a code that removes the need to analyze her arguments.'
  ]},
  {id: 'rivals-leibniz', title: 'Descartes, More, Hobbes, Spinoza—and the Leibniz comparison', paragraphs: [
    'Conway attacks Cartesian and Morean dualism because body and spirit cannot interact if their essential attributes exclude one another. She criticizes inert matter and the location of motion in dead substance. She groups Hobbes and Spinoza as materializing or confounding God and creation, although her reading does not reproduce every distinction in either thinker. Her polemics belong to a live seventeenth-century contest over substance, motion, divine action, and freedom. They show independent philosophical judgment: More’s former student retains his religious opposition to mechanism while rejecting his own solution.',
    'Leibniz owned a copy of the Principles, knew Van Helmont, and responded favorably to affinities between Conway’s living centers and his developing metaphysics. Her system can look monadological: living spiritual units, internal perception, organized bodies, continuity, and rejection of inert atoms. But the Principles should not be described as a draft of Leibniz’s Monadology. Conway’s Christological mediation, transmutation among degrees of body and spirit, creaturely composition, and account of restoration differ from Leibniz’s mature doctrines. Influence is plausible and historically connected; exact one-way borrowing for each parallel is harder to prove. Her text deserves interpretation before comparison.'
  ]},
  {id: 'reception-reading', title: 'Recovery, interpretive disputes, and a reading path', paragraphs: [
    'Anonymous posthumous publication limited Conway’s visibility, while histories organized around a few canonical men often treated her as a curiosity or source for Leibniz. Twentieth- and twenty-first-century scholarship recovered her as a systematic metaphysician. She now matters to histories of women philosophers, Cambridge Platonism, early modern vitalism, philosophy of religion, mind and body, process, and animal life. Recovery also produces new disagreements: whether created reality is one substance or many, whether “monism” is helpful, how persons persist through change, and how theological hierarchy relates to universal living continuity.',
    'Read the Principles by its nine chapters, beginning with divine attributes and the three species before approaching body, spirit, and transformation. Mark where the text argues from God’s goodness, where it criticizes named rivals, and where it introduces analogies of subtlety and composition. Use the Coudert–Corse edition for its translation and textual history, Hutton for the intellectual biography, and the SEP entry for a systematic map. Then compare Gordon-Roth’s monism analysis and Thomas on process. Keep a final rule: state Conway’s doctrine first, the publication mediation second, and Leibnizian resemblance third, so reception does not overwrite authorship.'
  ]},
];

