const astellArticle: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'orientation', title: 'Reason, education, and freedom inside a hierarchical world', paragraphs: [
    'Mary Astell asks a devastatingly simple question: if women possess rational souls capable of knowledge and virtue, why are they trained for display, dependence, and submission rather than disciplined judgment? Her answer joins educational reform, Cartesian and Augustinian philosophy, Christian devotion, critique of custom, and analysis of marriage. A Serious Proposal imagines institutions and methods through which women might cultivate their minds. Some Reflections upon Marriage exposes how unequal education and imprudent choice can turn marital authority into domination. These arguments made women’s intellectual agency impossible to dismiss as a private exception.',
    'It is tempting to translate Astell directly into a modern platform of equal rights. That recovers part of her challenge but erases its structure. She was a High Church Anglican and Tory defender of passive obedience, hierarchy, and religious authority; she criticized Locke and Whig contract arguments while deploying the language of slavery against arbitrary husbands. She did not advocate divorce, democratic rebellion, or the abolition of every gendered role. Her philosophy is powerful precisely because reason, freedom, obedience, and dependence pull against one another within it. A complete account presents those tensions rather than solving them on her behalf.',
    'Astell’s characteristic move is to expose a contradiction within accepted premises. If rational self-government is a human excellence, custom cannot excuse educating women into frivolity. If consent makes marriage binding, ignorance and coercive social expectations corrupt the judgment on which prudent consent depends. If obedience is owed to legitimate authority, a husband cannot turn limited household order into arbitrary mastery merely by invoking sex. These arguments do not produce a modern theory of equal citizenship, but neither are they ornamental complaints inside an unchanged hierarchy. They force readers to ask which forms of dependence can be reconciled with accountable agency and which merely train a subordinate to accept another person’s will. Her educational method matters because it supplies a practice of attention, definition, and self-examination through which that agency might actually be developed.'
  ]},
  {id: 'life-network', title: 'Newcastle, Chelsea, print, friendship, and patronage', paragraphs: [
    'Astell was born in Newcastle in 1666 into a Royalist Anglican family and received informal education, including instruction from an uncle. After family losses and limited prospects, she moved to the Chelsea area of London. There she developed networks of women readers, patrons, writers, and philanthropists, including Lady Catherine Jones and Lady Elizabeth Hastings, and corresponded philosophically with John Norris. She published anonymously, sometimes as “a Lover of her Sex,” in a print culture where a woman’s public authorship could itself become a target. Her career depended on friendship and patronage without being reducible to either.',
    'Astell never married. That fact informed her practical vantage without making Reflections a rejection of marriage as such. She participated in charity-school activity later in life and remained committed to Anglican worship and moral formation. Her books range beyond the two best-known works: Letters Concerning the Love of God debates Norris’s account of creaturely love; The Christian Religion offers philosophical and devotional argument; political pamphlets address dissent, conformity, rebellion, and the legitimacy of post-Revolution government. The familiar educational reformer was also a metaphysician, moral philosopher, controversial theologian, and polemical political writer.'
  ]},
  {id: 'rational-soul', title: 'The rational soul and the argument against natural inferiority', paragraphs: [
    'Astell’s case begins from the soul rather than from an empirical survey of talents. Human minds are thinking substances whose capacity for truth and virtue does not have a sex corresponding to bodily difference. If women appear frivolous, weak in judgment, or preoccupied with admiration, custom and education offer better explanations than a naturally defective rational soul. Society trains women to value beauty and compliance, denies sustained study, then treats the predictable outcome as proof that study would be wasted. The circularity is both epistemic and political: deprivation manufactures the evidence used to justify deprivation.',
    'Her metaphysics is broadly dualist and rationalist, yet not a simple copy of Descartes. God is the infinite first intelligence; created minds and bodies are finite; the embodied human being is a union whose passions and senses require discipline, not denial. Astell emphasizes the mind’s active attention and capacity to direct thought. This provides an account of agency under social pressure: custom can cloud judgment without destroying reason. The same framework can encourage mastery over bodily concerns in ways modern readers find ascetic. Its liberating claim is historically joined to a hierarchy that ranks intellectual and spiritual goods above many embodied attachments.'
  ]},
  {id: 'education-institution', title: 'The female religious retirement as school, refuge, and community', paragraphs: [
    'Part I of A Serious Proposal recommends a women’s religious retirement where residents could withdraw from corrupting social expectations, educate one another, cultivate friendship, and prepare either for a devoted unmarried life or wiser return to the world. The proposal resembles a convent in disciplined community while remaining framed for Protestant England. It is not simply a modern university with seventeenth-century decoration. Education aims at salvation and virtue; the institution regulates desire and sociability as well as teaching knowledge. Yet it also offers women economic and intellectual space not organized around courtship or dependence on husbands.',
    'The project serves several groups: women choosing religious study, those needing refuge from limited options, and those who would reenter family and society better able to judge. Astell sees friendship among women as intellectually productive rather than a diversion before marriage. The plan also reflects class limits. Its imagined residents are largely genteel women with access to resources, and it does not offer a universal public system addressing laboring women’s conditions. Recognizing that boundary does not cancel the innovation. It specifies whom Astell initially imagined as institution-builders and why later readers have both extended and criticized her educational politics.'
  ]},
  {id: 'method', title: 'Attention, clear ideas, definition, and the practice of reasoning', paragraphs: [
    'Part II turns from institutional vision to method. Astell urges readers to regulate attention, define terms, separate simple from complex ideas, avoid hasty judgment, and order inquiry from what is clear toward what is less known. Reason is a practice requiring habits and community, not a possession that automatically defeats prejudice. Rhetorical ornament and fashionable authority can conceal confusion. Women therefore need logic and method not to imitate pedantry but to test claims about religion, duty, and their own supposed incapacity. Intellectual self-government is a condition of responsible obedience rather than a license for impulsive contrarianism.',
    'Astell’s method draws on Descartes and the Port-Royal Logic while modifying them for ethical and educational aims. Knowledge involves perceiving relations among ideas; belief and testimony remain necessary where demonstration is unavailable. Authority is not intrinsically irrational, because credible testimony and revelation can guide finite knowers. The problem is servile deference that treats a human speaker’s status as a substitute for evidence. This balance explains why she can challenge male customs while defending church authority. Modern readers may doubt that the line is stable, but the tension is conceptual, not mere inconsistency: judgment must assess when submission is rationally and religiously due.'
  ]},
  {id: 'freedom-virtue', title: 'Freedom as rational self-command and orientation to the good', paragraphs: [
    'Freedom for Astell is not simply the ability to choose whatever one presently wants. Passions, vanity, fashion, flattery, and confused ideas can make a person dependent on external approval while she appears voluntary. Genuine freedom requires the understanding to recognize the good and the will to follow rational judgment. Education enlarges freedom by correcting the conditions under which choice is made. A woman trained only to secure a husband does not exercise fully independent judgment merely because she says yes; custom has narrowed the imaginable goods and made social survival depend on one institution.',
    'Virtue is therefore a form of ordered agency. Love should ultimately be directed toward God as the perfect good, while love of creatures must be proportioned and governed. Astell’s exchange with Norris presses the difficulty of loving created persons without idolatrous dependence. Friendship, charity, humility, and courage can support rational freedom, but pride can masquerade as self-respect and obedience can masquerade as virtue. The ethical program is demanding because domination operates internally as well as externally: escaping one arbitrary master is insufficient if appetite, applause, or doctrinal fashion still rules the mind.'
  ]},
  {id: 'marriage-consent', title: 'Consent under custom and the hazards of an irrevocable choice', paragraphs: [
    'Some Reflections upon Marriage insists that women should consider marriage with extraordinary care. Astell criticizes romantic flattery, parental and social pressure, inadequate knowledge of a prospective husband, and education that prepares women to attract rather than evaluate. Formal consent can coexist with ignorance and constrained alternatives. Once married, a wife faces legal and social subordination and, in Astell’s Christian account, a duty of obedience. The sharpest practical recommendation is therefore prospective: do not enter lightly an unequal relation from which the moral and legal exit is severely restricted.',
    'Astell does not condemn marriage itself. She describes Christian marriage as serving companionship, household order, and children when practiced with mutual virtue. Nor does she argue that every difference of authority is slavery. Her target is arbitrary power exercised for private appetite rather than the purposes that could justify office. The analogy between wife and subject exposes men who condemn political slavery while expecting domestic submission without accountability. Yet Astell stops short of deriving a general right of resistance for wives. The critique reveals domination powerfully while leaving obedience as the prescribed response after a valid marriage.'
  ]},
  {id: 'authority-politics', title: 'High Church Tory obedience and the anti-Lockean problem', paragraphs: [
    'Astell’s political pamphlets defend monarchy, the established church, and passive obedience against dissenting and Whig arguments after the Glorious Revolution. Passive obedience means obeying lawful authority where possible and submitting to penalties rather than actively resisting when commanded to sin. She attacks the ease with which private judgment can authorize rebellion and social disorder. Her political theology therefore differs sharply from later liberal narratives that make consent the continuous ground of government. Institutional authority has divine and historical claims that are not recreated whenever an individual reassesses advantage.',
    'This position creates the central interpretive tension. If arbitrary dominion is intolerable in marriage, why defend strong political obedience? Astell distinguishes offices, ends, jurisdictions, and the moral conduct of obedience; she can condemn abuse without treating authority as dissolved by abuse. Springborg reads her as a theorist of freedom from domination and a major critic of the contract analogy. Other readers stress the limits of a freedom secured within hierarchy. The page should not harmonize these views by declaring her secretly liberal or simply inconsistent. Her arguments test whether accountable authority and nonresistance can coexist—a problem she does not resolve to modern satisfaction.'
  ]},
  {id: 'religion-tensions', title: 'Reasoned faith, church authority, and the limits of emancipation', paragraphs: [
    'Religion supplies both the ground of Astell’s equality claim and the structure of her obedience. Rational souls share a capacity for knowing and loving God; no merely human custom can make women spiritual inferiors. Scripture and Christian virtue challenge vanity, cruelty, and arbitrary self-exaltation. At the same time, Astell defends Anglican orthodoxy, clerical and civil authority, and duties tied to hierarchical roles. Her proposal’s goal is not autonomous self-fashioning detached from God but freedom for rightly ordered devotion. Any account that removes theology removes the reasons she gives for education as well as the constraints she places on it.',
    'This produces genuine exclusions and dangers. Deference to established authority can protect existing inequalities; spiritualizing freedom can leave legal dependence intact; valorizing patient suffering can burden those harmed by husbands or rulers. Yet Astell’s insistence on examining authority with disciplined reason gives readers tools that can exceed her own political boundaries. The historical task is to keep both facts visible. She is not less philosophical because her conclusions are theological, and she is not more emancipatory than her texts because later feminism can extend her premises. Her importance lies partly in the friction between premise, method, and authorized conclusion.'
  ]},
  {id: 'reception-reading', title: 'Early feminist recovery and a reading path through the tensions', paragraphs: [
    'Astell has been called the first English feminist, an early feminist, a conservative feminist, a rationalist philosopher, and a theorist of freedom from domination. Each label illuminates something and risks making her a precursor whose value depends on resemblance to later movements. Her arguments directly challenge women’s educational and marital subordination, but her program differs from later rights-based, democratic, secular, intersectional, and economically broad feminisms. Her legacy is best understood as a powerful early-modern reorganization of reason, sex, custom, and authority whose internal tensions became resources for further criticism.',
    'Begin with A Serious Proposal Part I for the institutional argument, then Part II for method. Read Reflections as counsel and political analysis, attending to its prefaces and later revisions. Add selections from The Christian Religion and the political pamphlets before deciding how education, faith, and obedience fit together. The SEP and IEP entries provide philosophical maps; Springborg’s edition and monograph deepen the political conflict. Ask three questions throughout: what social condition distorts a woman’s choice, what kind of authority Astell regards as legitimate, and what remedy she actually permits. Those questions preserve her radical diagnoses and her restrictive conclusions in one honest portrait.'
  ]},
];

